import author_days_03_04 as a
a.STORIES.clear()

a.add(2,'dead-mans-deposit',"The Dead Man's Deposit",'an invented Leonida Keys marina escrow kiosk','Jason: navy overshirt, white tee, dark jeans; Lucia: olive rain jacket, black trousers','one refundable deposit receipt and a memorial plaque',
"The seller gave Jason a name to trust. Lucia found it on a memorial before paying.|They had left a deposit with the clerk for a boat neither had seen.|Their seller claimed the owner was away. He said the key and title would arrive after the final payment.|Lucia walked beneath the dock shelter to escape the rain. The same name shone on a brass plaque.|The owner had died last winter. His boat had become a community rescue launch. It wasn't for sale.|Jason called the seller. This time, the phone rang inside an empty tackle shop and nobody answered it.|They took their receipt back to the clerk. She checked the listing, returned their deposit, and reported the impersonation.|Outside, the rescue launch crossed the channel. Jason watched their imaginary getaway boat bring somebody safely home instead.",
"Lucia studies brass memorial plaque, Jason holds receipt beside her|Earlier marina kiosk, anonymous clerk gives refundable deposit receipt|Jason reads sale message, no real boat key or title shown|Lucia shelters beside memorial plaque in warm light|Community rescue launch moored across channel, clearly separate vessel|Jason calls phone outside closed empty tackle shop|Clerk refunds deposit into Jason palm against original receipt|Rescue launch returns with anonymous rescued adult; heroes watch from public shore",
'memorial identity exposes impersonation; protected deposit returned',timeline='nonlinear: discovered name then earlier deposit and return to discovery')

a.add(2,'empty-bag-premium','The Empty Bag Premium','an invented Leonida Keys closed resort luggage desk','Jason: charcoal work jacket, grey tee, jeans; Lucia: burgundy shirt, black waterproof trousers','one empty hand-stitched leather satchel with crescent-shaped repair and one payment envelope',
"Jason delivered an empty bag and waited for accusations. The buyer hugged it before checking a single pocket.|Lucia had insisted they leave the contents alone. There weren't any. That made Jason mistrust the job.|Their anonymous client had offered real money for a worn satchel from a shuttered resort's lost property.|At the desk, the buyer traced a crescent repair beneath its handle. His father had stitched that crooked seam.|Jason glanced over. She had spotted the initials inside the repair and checked with the clerk.|The bag had been held for years. Its owner hadn't known the resort was closing until morning.|He paid their agreed fee and walked out carrying nothing else. Jason opened the envelope. Every bill was there.|Lucia asked whether an empty bag still seemed worthless. Jason put their fee away without answering.",
"Jason places open empty satchel on luggage desk; anonymous buyer embraces it|Earlier Jason opens empty satchel, Lucia gestures toward untouched lining|Heroes examine lost property tag, shuttered resort lobby behind|Buyer fingers crescent seam gently, satchel remains empty|Flashback insert initials stitched beneath repair, Lucia at desk with clerk|Clerk shows old intake date on property ledger beside satchel|Buyer departs holding satchel, Jason opens payment envelope at desk|Jason pockets closed payment envelope, Lucia watches near exit",
'object value resides in family repair rather than hidden contents',timeline='nonlinear: buyer reaction, short explicit earlier recovery, return to payment')

a.add(2,'borrowed-sunset','The Borrowed Sunset','an invented Leonida Keys waterfront snack terrace','Jason: tan shirt, charcoal raincoat, jeans; Lucia: teal jacket, black tee, dark trousers','one phone showing a paused sunset video and one untouched yellow payment envelope',
"Someone offered Lucia cash to remember a sunset she'd never seen. Jason thought yes would cost nothing.|The man played a video: orange water, a blue railing, himself smiling beside an empty chair.|He wanted her to say she'd sat there yesterday. One harmless sentence, he said, sliding over money.|Lucia knew the terrace. Its railing had been repainted white last month. The man stopped smiling.|Jason looked at the envelope, then video. Somebody else might pay for their convenient memory with years behind bars.|He pushed the cash back. The man called them ungrateful and walked away without another word.|They bought one coffee to share beneath the real white railing while rain erased the last orange light offshore.|Jason raised the cup toward the empty chair. That one sentence, Lucia said, would have belonged to somebody forever.",
"Lucia faces anonymous man at terrace table; yellow envelope between them|Phone screen depicts old blue railing and orange sunset, no Lucia|Man slides closed envelope toward Lucia, Jason seated beside her|Lucia points at actual white terrace railing behind phone|Jason looks between untouched envelope and paused phone|Jason slides unopened envelope back; man takes phone and cash away|Heroes share single coffee at same terrace, white railing, storm dusk|Jason raises single cup toward empty third chair, Lucia holds gaze",
'physical setting change exposes an attempted purchased alibi')

a.add(2,'the-torn-map','The Torn Map','an invented Leonida Keys island land-records waiting room','Jason: green canvas jacket, white shirt, jeans; Lucia: rust raincoat, black trousers','one torn hand-drawn shoreline map and full public survey displayed behind glass',
"Half a map cost Jason five hundred. The other half hung behind Lucia.|A seller had promised buried money beneath an island house. The second piece would cost another thousand.|Jason arrived at the land office to identify the shore. Lucia went looking for a restroom.|She found the complete survey beside a drinking fountain. Their secret mark wasn't treasure. It was a septic tank.|The seller had traced a public drawing and torn it to invent mystery. Jason stared, cooling.|They couldn't recover the cash then. They could stop the next sale. Lucia photographed both drawings for the complaint.|Outside, the seller called to ask whether they wanted the ending. Jason said he'd already found it beside a fountain.|Then he bought Lucia dinner with what remained. For five hundred, he'd learned the difference between hidden and simply unread.",
"Lucia beside full framed survey, Jason holds matching torn map|Earlier Jason studies torn map at office entry, phone offer visible|Jason waits near records desk while Lucia follows restroom sign|Lucia finds complete shoreline survey by drinking fountain, septic symbol clear as added overlay|Jason compares torn sheet to framed survey through glass|Lucia photographs both drawings for complaint; no private data|Outside Jason declines caller, Lucia holds folded torn map|Heroes share inexpensive dinner under takeaway awning, map in Jason pocket",
'manufactured scarcity and public information defeat treasure scam')

a.add(2,'the-last-meter','The Last Meter','an invented Leonida Keys causeway taxi shelter','Jason: grey hoodie, black rain jacket, dark jeans; Lucia: cream shirt, navy rain shell, black trousers','one metered yellow taxi with black steel rims, one fare receipt and returned envelope',
"Jason thought the driver had sold them out. Why else stop the taxi with their destination still ahead?|An anonymous man waited under the shelter, holding the envelope their buyer had refused. Jason reached for his door.|The driver paused the meter, and pointed toward Lucia's seat. Her phone was lying beneath it.|She'd dropped it. The waiting man was a dispatcher, carrying the envelope they'd left at the taxi office.|Jason relaxed his hand. He'd been preparing for somebody to take something. Nobody had even tried.|Lucia collected her phone. Jason checked the envelope in front of them. Their returned advance was still intact.|He paid the fare and added a tip. The driver smiled and asked if they wanted their destination.|Home, Lucia said. The meter started again. Jason watched its numbers climb and felt relieved to understand why.",
"Yellow taxi stops at lit shelter, Jason driver-side rear passenger, Lucia passenger-side rear, anonymous driver front left|Anonymous dispatcher waits under shelter holding returned envelope; Jason touches interior door handle|Driver points safely from parked seat toward phone under Lucia rear seat|Lucia retrieves own phone, dispatcher hands envelope through open rear window|Jason relaxes empty open palm beside rear seat|Jason opens returned advance envelope, banknotes stay inside; Lucia holds phone|Jason pays driver from separate wallet, tip visible in open palm|Taxi departs with same driver and heroes rear seats, dispatcher stays shelter, tires contact wet road",
'suspicious detour resolves as honest lost-property service')

a.add(2,'the-fourth-chair','The Fourth Chair','an invented Leonida Keys motel meeting porch','Jason: olive shirt, dark denim jacket, jeans; Lucia: maroon raincoat, black trousers','four folding chairs, three empty coffee cups and one unsealed payment envelope',
"I stared at the fourth chair. Nobody mentioned another person at our meeting.|Jason and I had come to settle a debt. Our creditor placed four chairs beneath the motel awning.|Three coffees cooled on the table. He kept checking the road. Jason counted the money again, quietly.|I asked who else was coming. The man said his son. He wanted a witness when we paid him back.|I thought witness meant leverage. Then he said his son believed every promise broke. Tonight, he'd show otherwise.|We waited. A young adult arrived soaked and angry. Jason slid full payment across the table.|He counted once, thanked us, and handed back our signed debt note. His son watched him do it.|I took the fourth chair while they talked. We had arrived expecting somebody else to disappoint us that night.",
"Lucia eyes empty fourth folding chair on porch, Jason beside her|Earlier anonymous creditor arranges four chairs, three cups on table|Creditor checks road, Jason counts notes discreetly inside open envelope|Lucia asks creditor, empty fourth chair kept at right|Creditor gestures gently toward road; heroes listen|Anonymous adult son arrives in raincoat, Jason slides payment across table|Creditor returns signed paid note, adult son watches|All four seated, empty envelope by Jason, paid note held by Lucia",
'a feared witness becomes a witness to a promise kept',pov='first person: Lucia',voice='Laura')
a.STORIES[-1]['delivery']='first-person confession'

a.add(2,'closed-for-weather','Closed for Weather','an invented Leonida Keys glass-roofed seaplane ticket kiosk','Jason: blue shirt, charcoal jacket, jeans; Lucia: amber rain shell, black trousers','one closed cash envelope, two refundable excursion tickets, storm cancellation sign',
"We paid for two seats. There's the plane, there's our pilot, and he's drinking coffee.|Look past him, Jason. The storm isn't over the island yet. It's sitting across the route we'd be taking.|Our buyer leaves tonight. If we're absent, he keeps the deal. Tell me there's another flight.|There isn't. There is a cancellation notice, and it says refunds at this counter. Keep them dry.|I hate losing a score because somebody who doesn't know us decides the sky's wrong. We could ask him.|You can. But ask him what he sees, instead of asking what he'd charge to ignore it.|He says that black line hides the horizon. I didn't even know I was looking at a line.|Neither did I. Take the refund. We'll buy breakfast where the floor stays still, and complain about the weather.",
"Jason addresses Lucia inside glass-roofed kiosk, stationary floatplane moored outside, anonymous pilot drinks coffee on shore|Lucia points to distant storm wall beyond secured plane|Jason holds closed cash envelope and two excursion tickets|Lucia points to cancellation sign and refund counter|Jason glances toward shore pilot, envelope remains closed|Lucia calmly faces Jason, pilot in distant background|Jason returns from brief offscreen weather question, looks at obscured horizon|Lucia receives refund for tickets at kiosk counter, Jason waits beside her",
'weather perspective defeats pressure to purchase unsafe passage')
a.STORIES[-1]['delivery']='character dialogue'
a.STORIES[-1]['pov']='two-character external POV: only what Jason and Lucia say or observe'

